## Rust quote! Macro Interpolation Restriction
When using Rust's quote! macro, the `#` interpolation only accepts direct local variable references. When you need to pass an expression (e.g., `trait_paths[true]`), first assign it to a local variable and then reference that variable inside `quote!`.

Example:
```rust
// ❌ Wrong: Direct expression interpolation
quote! {
    impl<#t: #(&trait_paths[false])> #(&trait_paths[false]) for T { }
}

// ✅ Correct: Assign to local variable first
let view_trait_path = &trait_paths[false];
quote! {
    impl<#t: #view_trait_path> #view_trait_path for T { }
}
```
